# Theory — Move Semantics & Rvalue References

## 1. Value Categories: lvalue vs rvalue

| Category | Has address? | Example |
|----------|-------------|----------|
| lvalue | ✅ Yes | `x`, `arr[0]`, `*ptr` |
| rvalue | ❌ No | `42`, `x+1`, `Widget()`, `std::move(x)` |
| xvalue (expiring) | ✅ but moveable | `std::move(x)` |

An rvalue reference (`T&&`) binds *only* to rvalues and xvalues — it is the mechanism that enables the move constructor.

## 2. Move Constructor

A move constructor *steals* resources from the source, leaving it in a valid but empty state:

```cpp
class Buffer {
    int* data_;
    std::size_t size_;
public:
    // Move constructor — O(1), just pointer swap
    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept
        : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_) {
        other.data_ = nullptr;  // leave source in valid state
        other.size_ = 0;
    }
};
```

Always mark move operations `noexcept` — the standard library relies on this to choose move over copy in containers like `std::vector`.

## 3. `std::move` — It Doesn't Move Anything

`std::move` is just a **cast to an rvalue reference**:

```cpp
template<typename T>
constexpr std::remove_reference_t<T>&& move(T&& t) noexcept {
    return static_cast<std::remove_reference_t<T>&&>(t);
}
```

The *actual movement* happens in the move constructor/assignment that gets called. `std::move` just tells the compiler "I'm done with this object, move from it."

## 4. Move Assignment

```cpp
Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& other) noexcept {
    if (this != &other) {
        delete[] data_;           // free our own resource first
        data_ = other.data_;      // steal
        size_ = other.size_;
        other.data_ = nullptr;    // reset source
        other.size_ = 0;
    }
    return *this;
}
```

## 5. Copy Elision / NRVO

C++17 mandates **prvalue copy elision**: when you return a local object by value, the compiler constructs it *directly in the caller's storage* — no copy, no move:

```cpp
Buffer makeBuffer(std::size_t n) {
    Buffer b(n);   // constructed directly at call site (C++17)
    return b;      // NRVO applies even in C++11/14
}
```

Do **not** write `return std::move(b)` — it defeats NRVO.

## 6. Perfect Forwarding

Perfect forwarding preserves the value category through a template:

```cpp
template<typename T>
void wrapper(T&& arg) {
    process(std::forward<T>(arg));  // lvalue if T=T&, rvalue if T=T
}
```

`std::forward<T>` is *conditional move* — it only casts to rvalue if the original argument was an rvalue.

## Self-Check

- Why must move operations be `noexcept` for `std::vector` to use them?
- What happens to the source after `std::move`?
- When does NRVO *not* apply?

