# Pitfalls — Move Semantics & Rvalue References

## ❌ Pitfall 1: Using an Object After Moving From It

```cpp
std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};
auto w = std::move(v);
std::cout << v.size();  // ❌ v is valid but unspecified state
```

After a move, the source is **valid but unspecified**. Don't read from it unless you reset it explicitly.

## ❌ Pitfall 2: `return std::move(local)` — Defeats NRVO

```cpp
// ❌ Forces a move instead of free elision
Widget makeWidget() {
    Widget w;
    return std::move(w);   // disables NRVO!
}

// ✅ Trust the compiler
Widget makeWidget() {
    Widget w;
    return w;              // NRVO: zero copies, zero moves
}
```

## ❌ Pitfall 3: Forgetting `noexcept` on Move Operations

```cpp
class Foo {
public:
    Foo(Foo&& other) { ... }           // ❌ no noexcept
    Foo& operator=(Foo&& other) { ... } // ❌ no noexcept
};

std::vector<Foo> v;
v.push_back(Foo{});  // vector COPIES Foo — cannot use move without noexcept!
```

Without `noexcept`, `std::vector::push_back` cannot use the move constructor and falls back to copying.

## ❌ Pitfall 4: `std::move` on `const` Objects

```cpp
const std::string s = "hello";
auto t = std::move(s);  // ❌ calls COPY constructor — const can't be moved!
```

Moving from `const` silently degrades to a copy.

## ❌ Pitfall 5: Moving in a Range-For Loop

```cpp
for (auto& item : collection) {
    process(std::move(item));  // ❌ collection now full of moved-from objects!
}
```

Move elements from a container only if you're done with the whole container.

## Prevention Checklist

- Always mark move operations `noexcept`
- Do NOT `return std::move(local)` — trust NRVO
- After `std::move(x)`, treat `x` as default-constructed
- Use `-Wpessimizing-move` (Clang) to catch NRVO killers
- Run with ASan + UBSan to catch use-after-move

